{
  "title": "✏️ Dividing a Line Segment in a Given Ratio",
  "hook": "You have a line segment AB and you need to find the point that divides it in ratio 3:2 — but you can only use a compass and straightedge! This classical construction has been used since Euclid's time.",
  "explanation": "<div style=\"background:#F3E5F5;border-left:4px solid #7B1FA2;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0\"><strong style=\"color:#7B1FA2\">Problem:</strong> Divide a given line segment AB in a given ratio m:n using only compass and straightedge.</div><p><b>Construction Steps (to divide AB in ratio m:n):</b></p><div style=\"margin:12px 0\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#1565C0;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">1</div><div><strong>Draw ray AX at any acute angle to AB</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">AX should not overlap AB</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#1565C0;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">2</div><div><strong>Mark (m+n) equal arcs on AX</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">Using compass, mark points A₁, A₂, ..., A_(m+n) on AX</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#1565C0;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">3</div><div><strong>Join the last point to B</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">Join A_(m+n) to B</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#1565C0;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">4</div><div><strong>Through A_m, draw line parallel to A_(m+n)B</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">This line meets AB at point P</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#1565C0;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">5</div><div><strong>P divides AB in ratio m:n</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">AP:PB = m:n</span></div></div></div><div style=\"background:#F5F5F5;border-left:3px solid #4A90D9;border-radius:6px;padding:8px 12px;margin:6px 0;font-size:0.9rem\"><b>Why it works:</b> By BPT (Basic Proportionality Theorem), a line parallel to one side of a triangle divides the other two sides proportionally.</div><div style=\"background:#FFF8E1;border-left:4px solid #FFB300;border-radius:8px;padding:10px 14px;margin:10px 0;font-size:0.88rem\">💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> Alternative: You can also use the section formula to verify: if A=(0,0) and B=(a,0), then P = (ma/(m+n), 0).</div>",
  "worked_example": "<b>Example:</b> Divide a line segment of 7 cm in ratio 2:3.<br><br><div style=\"margin:12px 0\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">1</div><div><strong>Draw AB = 7 cm</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\"></span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">2</div><div><strong>Draw ray AX at acute angle to AB</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">Any angle works, ~30° is convenient</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">3</div><div><strong>Mark 5 (=2+3) equal arcs on AX</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">A₁, A₂, A₃, A₄, A₅</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">4</div><div><strong>Join A₅ to B</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\"></span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">5</div><div><strong>Through A₂, draw A₂P ∥ A₅B</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">P is on AB</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">6</div><div><strong>Result: AP = 2.8 cm, PB = 4.2 cm</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">AP/PB = 2.8/4.2 = 2/3 ✓</span></div></div></div>",
  "try_this": {
